<section class="pl-hero"><div class="pl-r pl-hero-t"><p class="pl-sm">An independent dinner companion</p><h1>Everything hot. Everyone seated.</h1><p class="pl-in">Platter is about the hardest part of dinner: getting it all to the table at once. Plan the timings, scale a recipe to your table and build a weeknight plate in under a minute.</p><p><a class="btn" href="#kitchen">Plan tonight&rsquo;s dinner</a></p></div>
<div class="pl-r pl-plates"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1764756994472-e39e36c43ffe?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=520&q=75" alt="plate of chicken and vegetables" width="520" height="520" fetchpriority="high"></figure><figure class="pl-mid"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1612982784130-e68f66f34974?auto=format&fit=crop&w=620&h=620&q=75" alt="pasta dish on a white ceramic plate" width="620" height="620" fetchpriority="high"></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1727882368739-23905cdb08f2?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=520&q=75" alt="white plate topped with green vegetables" width="520" height="520" fetchpriority="high"></figure></div></section>

<section class="pl-s"><div class="pl-r pl-form"><div class="pl-pie" role="img" aria-label="Plate divided into half vegetables, a quarter protein and a quarter starch"><span>&frac12;<small>veg</small></span><span>&frac14;<small>protein</small></span><span>&frac14;<small>starch</small></span></div><div><p class="pl-sm">The plate formula</p><h2>Half, a quarter, a quarter</h2><p>You do not need a recipe to plan a balanced dinner. Fill half the plate with vegetables, a quarter with protein and a quarter with something starchy. Every weeknight idea on this site is built on that shape.</p><ul class="pl-dots"><li><b>Half:</b> two vegetables if you can, one cooked and one raw or lightly dressed.</li><li><b>Quarter:</b> chicken, fish, eggs, beans, lentils, tofu or meat, about the size of your palm.</li><li><b>Quarter:</b> potatoes, rice, pasta, bread or grains, about a cupped handful cooked.</li></ul></div></div></section>

<section class="pl-s pl-sage" id="kitchen"><div class="pl-r"><p class="pl-sm pl-ctr">Kitchen tools</p><h2 class="pl-ctr">Three things worth working out before you cook</h2><div class="pl-kit">
<div class="pl-card pl-tall"><h3>Dinner Timing Planner</h3><p>Set the time you want to sit down, then list each dish.</p><label class="pl-serve" for="tm-s">Serve dinner at<input id="tm-s" type="time" value="19:00"></label>
<div class="pl-rows"><div class="pl-rowh"><span>Dish</span><span>Cook (min)</span><span>Rest (min)</span></div>
<div class="pl-row"><input aria-label="Dish 1 name" class="tm-n" type="text" value="Roast chicken"><input aria-label="Dish 1 cooking minutes" class="tm-c" type="number" min="0" max="600" value="80"><input aria-label="Dish 1 resting minutes" class="tm-r" type="number" min="0" max="120" value="15"></div>
<div class="pl-row"><input aria-label="Dish 2 name" class="tm-n" type="text" value="Roast potatoes"><input aria-label="Dish 2 cooking minutes" class="tm-c" type="number" min="0" max="600" value="50"><input aria-label="Dish 2 resting minutes" class="tm-r" type="number" min="0" max="120" value="0"></div>
<div class="pl-row"><input aria-label="Dish 3 name" class="tm-n" type="text" value="Green beans"><input aria-label="Dish 3 cooking minutes" class="tm-c" type="number" min="0" max="600" value="8"><input aria-label="Dish 3 resting minutes" class="tm-r" type="number" min="0" max="120" value="0"></div>
<div class="pl-row"><input aria-label="Dish 4 name" class="tm-n" type="text" value="Gravy"><input aria-label="Dish 4 cooking minutes" class="tm-c" type="number" min="0" max="600" value="10"><input aria-label="Dish 4 resting minutes" class="tm-r" type="number" min="0" max="120" value="0"></div></div>
<div class="pl-out" id="tm-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="pl-card"><h3>Portion Scaler</h3><p>Resize any ingredient for the number at your table.</p><div class="pl-f2"><label for="ps-a">Recipe serves<input id="ps-a" type="number" min="1" max="100" value="4" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="ps-b">I am cooking for<input id="ps-b" type="number" min="1" max="100" value="7" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="ps-q">Ingredient amount<input id="ps-q" type="number" min="0" step="any" value="300" inputmode="decimal"></label><label for="ps-u">Unit<select id="ps-u"><option>g</option><option>ml</option><option>tbsp</option><option>tsp</option><option>cups</option><option>pieces</option></select></label></div><div class="pl-out" id="ps-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="pl-card"><h3>Weeknight Plate Builder</h3><p>Tell it what you have and how long you have got.</p><div class="pl-f2"><label for="wp-t">Time<select id="wp-t"><option value="20">20 minutes</option><option value="40" selected>40 minutes</option><option value="60">An hour</option></select></label><label for="wp-m">Main<select id="wp-m"><option value="chicken">Chicken</option><option value="fish">Fish</option><option value="beans">Beans or lentils</option><option value="eggs">Eggs</option><option value="mince">Mince</option><option value="veg">Vegetables only</option></select></label><label class="pl-full" for="wp-k">I would rather use<select id="wp-k"><option value="pan">One frying pan</option><option value="tray">One oven tray</option><option value="pot">One pot</option></select></label></div><div class="pl-out" id="wp-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="pl-s"><div class="pl-r"><p class="pl-sm">A loose rotation</p><h2>Seven nights, seven shapes</h2><p class="pl-in">Deciding what to cook is often harder than cooking it. Give each night a shape, not a recipe, and the decision is half made.</p><ol class="pl-week"><li><span>Mon</span><b>Something on rice</b>A stir-fry or a quick curry.</li><li><span>Tue</span><b>Traybake</b>Protein and vegetables on one tray.</li><li><span>Wed</span><b>Pasta</b>Sauce made while the water boils.</li><li><span>Thu</span><b>Eggs</b>Frittata, shakshuka or omelettes.</li><li><span>Fri</span><b>Fish</b>Pan-fried, with potatoes and greens.</li><li><span>Sat</span><b>Slow pot</b>A stew or braise with time to spare.</li><li><span>Sun</span><b>Roast</b>And leftovers for Monday.</li></ol></div></section>

<section class="pl-s pl-plum"><div class="pl-r"><p class="pl-sm pl-ctr">Read before you cook</p><h2 class="pl-ctr">Two guides</h2><div class="pl-gds"><a class="pl-gd" href="dinner-timing-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1757451628319-60463e4fede3?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=420&q=75" alt="diced sweet potatoes and onions with rosemary" width="640" height="420" loading="lazy"><div><h3>The Dinner Timing Guide</h3><p>Working backwards from the table: start times, resting, holding food warm and what to do when one dish runs late.</p><span>Read the guide</span></div></a><a class="pl-gd pl-gd2" href="weeknight-dinner-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1779939855792-9c52a79d77fc?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=420&q=75" alt="prepared meal of meatballs and sweet potatoes" width="640" height="420" loading="lazy"><div><h3>The Weeknight Dinner Guide</h3><p>A method for cooking five dinners a week without five recipes: shapes, a short shopping list and Sunday prep.</p><span>Read the guide</span></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="pl-s"><div class="pl-r pl-rest"><div><p class="pl-sm">Patience pays</p><h2>Resting times worth memorising</h2><p>Resting is cooking time you get back. While the meat sits, the oven is free for crisping potatoes and the hob is free for gravy.</p></div><div class="tblw"><table class="pl-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Dish</th><th>Rest for</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Steak, chops, chicken breast</td><td>5&ndash;10 min</td><td>On a warm plate</td></tr><tr><td>Whole roast chicken</td><td>15&ndash;20 min</td><td>Loosely covered with foil</td></tr><tr><td>Roast beef or lamb joint</td><td>20&ndash;30 min</td><td>Covered, on a board with a groove</td></tr><tr><td>Lasagne and baked pasta</td><td>10&ndash;15 min</td><td>Uncovered, to set</td></tr><tr><td>Rice</td><td>5&ndash;10 min</td><td>Lid on, heat off</td></tr><tr><td>Fish</td><td>None</td><td>Serve at once</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="pl-s pl-cream"><div class="pl-r pl-tab"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1764265928338-3b9960b07397?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=640&q=75" alt="woman eating a meal at an outdoor table" width="640" height="640" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="pl-sm">Before you call everyone</p><h2>The last five minutes</h2><ol class="pl-last"><li>Warm the plates in a low oven or under hot water.</li><li>Put water, glasses and cutlery on the table.</li><li>Taste every dish once more and adjust the salt.</li><li>Carve or portion in the kitchen, not at the table.</li><li>Serve the hottest thing last, straight from the pan.</li></ol></div></div></section>

<section class="pl-s"><div class="pl-r pl-fq"><p class="pl-sm pl-ctr">Dinner questions</p><h2 class="pl-ctr">Asked at the stove</h2><div class="pl-faq"><details open><summary>How do I get every dish ready at the same time?</summary><p>Work backwards from the moment you want to sit down. Subtract each dish&rsquo;s resting time and cooking time to find when it must start, then cook in that order. The timing planner above does the subtraction for you.</p></details><details><summary>How long should meat rest before carving?</summary><p>A whole roast chicken or a joint needs fifteen to twenty minutes, loosely covered. Steaks and chops need five to ten. Resting lets the juices settle, and it gives you time to finish the vegetables and gravy.</p></details><details><summary>What is a balanced dinner plate?</summary><p>A useful rule is half the plate vegetables, a quarter protein and a quarter starchy food such as potatoes, rice or pasta. It works for almost any cuisine and needs no weighing.</p></details><details><summary>Can I double any recipe?</summary><p>Most stews, soups, sauces and traybakes double well if you use a wider pan or two trays. Baking is less forgiving, and seasoning should be increased cautiously: add about one and a half times, taste, then adjust.</p></details><details><summary>What can I cook in thirty minutes on a weeknight?</summary><p>Anything that cooks fast over high heat or needs no attention: a stir-fry, pan-fried fish with quick vegetables, an omelette, or pasta with a sauce made while the water boils.</p></details><details><summary>How do I keep food warm without drying it out?</summary><p>Hold cooked dishes in an oven at about 70&deg;C, covered with foil or a lid, for up to thirty minutes. Warm the plates in the same oven; a hot plate keeps food warm at the table for much longer.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="pl-s pl-sage pl-cardsec"><div class="pl-r"><div class="pl-invite"><p class="pl-sm">By email, monthly</p><h2>The Platter Card</h2><p>One dinner shape, one timing tip and one thing to cook ahead. Short enough to read while the kettle boils.</p><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@platter.com?subject=The%20Platter%20Card&amp;body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></div></div></section>

